  "difficulty": 1,
  "prompt": "How does the graph of $y = \\sin\\left(x - \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$ compare with the graph of $y = \\sin(x)$?",
  "answerType": "multiple-choice",
  "choices": [
    {
      "id": "A",
      "label": "Shifted right by $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$",
      "correct": true
    },
    {
      "id": "B",
      "label": "Shifted left by $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$",
      "correct": false
    },
    {
      "id": "C",
      "label": "Shifted down by $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$",
      "correct": false
    },
    {
      "id": "D",
      "label": "Stretched horizontally by $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$",
      "correct": false
    }
  ],
  "primaryAnswer": "A",
  "alternativeAnswers": [],
  "acceptanceMode": "exact",
  "hint": "A change inside the parentheses moves the graph sideways, not up and down.",
  "hints": [
    {
      "level": "nudge",
      "text": "A change inside the parentheses moves the graph sideways, not up and down."
    },
    {
      "level": "guide",
      "text": "Ask where the curve now starts its cycle: you need $x - \\frac{\\pi}{3} = 0$."
    },
    {
      "level": "reveal",
      "text": "$x = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$, so the cycle begins $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ later — a shift to the right."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": [
    "Subtracting inside the function moves the graph horizontally.",
    "The cycle used to start where the input was $0$, which was $x = 0$; now the input is $0$ when $x = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$.",
    "So every feature of the wave happens $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ later: the graph slides right by $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$."
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "The minus sign feels backwards on purpose",
      "steps": [
        "A minus inside always shifts right, and a plus inside always shifts left.",
        "Think of it as the graph running late: it has to wait until $x$ reaches $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ before it can do what it used to do at $0$."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "tags": [
    "phase-shift",
    "horizontal-translation"
  ],
